@DzmitryShylovich you were right to ask, the problem is not the one I thought it was.

Simply add such a component:
```
export interface IInterface { }

@Component({
  selector: 'app-component',
  template: '<div></div>'
})
export class BugComponent {
  constructor(
    public data: { prop: IInterface }
    ) { }
}
```

to an existing project and add the component to the `declarations` of a `NgModule` and it will show the bug.

To reproduce:
```sh
$ mkdir bug
$ cd bug
$ ng new
$ cat << EOF | git apply
diff --git a/src/app/app.component.ts b/src/app/app.component.ts
index ff63e05..ddbf7a1 100644
--- a/src/app/app.component.ts
+++ b/src/app/app.component.ts
@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@
-import { Component } from '@angular/core';
+import { Component, Inject } from '@angular/core';

 @Component({
   selector: 'app-root',
@@ -8,3 +8,15 @@ import { Component } from '@angular/core';
 export class AppComponent {
   title = 'app works!';
 }
+
+export interface IInterface { }
+
+@Component({
+  selector: 'app-component',
+  template: '<div></div>'
+})
+export class BugComponent {
+  constructor(
+    public data: { prop: IInterface }
+    ) { }
+}
diff --git a/src/app/app.module.ts b/src/app/app.module.ts
index 67ae491..d43010b 100644
--- a/src/app/app.module.ts
+++ b/src/app/app.module.ts
@@ -3,11 +3,12 @@ import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
 import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
 import { HttpModule } from '@angular/http';

-import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
+import { AppComponent, BugComponent } from './app.component';

 @NgModule({
   declarations: [
-    AppComponent
+    AppComponent,
+    BugComponent
   ],
   imports: [
     BrowserModule,
EOF
```

@DzmitryShylovich in my real use case, the component's constructor parameter is declared like this:
```ts
constructor(
    public dialogRef: MdDialogRef<WorkspaceDeleteDialogComponent>,
    // TODO add some type for data when https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/15424 is fixed
    @Inject(MD_DIALOG_DATA) public data: any
  ) { }
```

And I create the dialog like this:
```ts
    this.dialog.open(WorkspaceDeleteDialogComponent, {
      data: { workspace$: this.workspace$ }
    })
```

And `@angular/material` takes care of injecting `data` into my parameter.

@DzmitryShylovich sorry, I made a mistake in my last comment, I meant the problem was with:
```ts
constructor(
    public dialogRef: MdDialogRef<WorkspaceDeleteDialogComponent>,
    @Inject(MD_DIALOG_DATA) public data: { workspace$: Observable<IWorkspace>} 
  ) { }
```

It does works if I use `@Inject(MD_DIALOG_DATA) public data: any` since it does not have an interface to type the parameter.

create an interface
```
export interface DialogData {
  workspace$: Observable<IWorkspace>
}

@Inject(MD_DIALOG_DATA) public data: DialogData 
```

I have an same issue with firebase injection

```
import {AngularFire, FirebaseApp} from 'angularfire2';
@Injectable()
export class LoggerService {
  constructor(@Inject(FirebaseApp) firebaseApp: firebase.app.App) {
    this.storage_ref = firebaseApp.storage().ref();
```

This code is used for firebase function which is not suported by Angularfire2. ie: FirebaseStorage.
Is there also work around way for it?
